+\subsection{A Genuine Minimum-Rank-Two Boundary-Typed Target}
+\label{subsec:genuine-ranktwo}
+
+The higher-rank characteristic-data theorem is not only a uniform statement
+over presentations.  The following target has minimum rule rank two already at
+fan-out two, belongs to the simplest nontrivial boundary-typed class, and lies
+outside Yoshinaka's trivial-typing baseline.
+
+Let
+\[
+ \Sigma_{\mathrm{r2}}
+ :=\{\mathtt a_i,\mathtt b_i:1\le i\le4\}
+   \cup\{\mathtt c_1\}
+   \cup\{\mathtt{\#}_0,\mathtt{\#}_1,\mathtt{\#}_2,
+          \mathtt{\#}_3,\mathtt{\#}_4\},
+\]
+where the five separator symbols are distinct, and define
+\[
+\begin{aligned}
+ L_{\mathrm{r2}}
+ :=\bigl\{\, &
+ \mathtt{\#}_0
+ \mathtt a_1^{n_1}\mathtt b_1^{n_1}\mathtt c_1^{n_1}\mathtt{\#}_1
+ \mathtt a_2^{n_2}\mathtt b_2^{n_2}\mathtt{\#}_2\\
+ &{}\mathtt a_3^{n_3}\mathtt b_3^{n_3}\mathtt{\#}_3
+ \mathtt a_4^{n_4}\mathtt b_4^{n_4}\mathtt{\#}_4
+ :n_1,n_2,n_3,n_4\ge1
+ \,\bigr\}.
+\end{aligned}
+\]
+
+\begin{theorem}[A genuinely rank-two typed witness]
+\label{thm:genuine-ranktwo-witness}
+The language $L_{\mathrm{r2}}$ satisfies
+\[
+ L_{\mathrm{r2}}
+ \in
+ \Cmcf{2}{h_{\Sigma_{\mathrm{r2}}}^{1,1}}
+ \cap\mathsf{MCFL}(2,2),
+ \qquad
+ L_{\mathrm{r2}}\notin\mathsf{MCFL}(2,1),
+ \qquad
+ L_{\mathrm{r2}}\notin\YSub{2}.
+\]
+Consequently, its minimum rule rank among fan-out-two MCFG presentations is
+exactly two.  In particular,
+Theorem~\ref{thm:boundary-polydata} applies to a language that cannot be
+covered by Theorem~\ref{thm:rankone-polydata}.
+\end{theorem}
+
+\begin{proof}
+First, $L_{\mathrm{r2}}\in\mathsf{MCFL}(2,2)$.  Use fan-out-two
+nonterminals $A_1,\ldots,A_4$, fan-out-one nonterminals
+$B_1,\ldots,B_4$, and a fan-out-one start symbol $S$.  Put
+\[
+\begin{aligned}
+ A_1&\to(\mathtt a_1\mathtt b_1,\mathtt c_1\mathtt{\#}_1)
+   \mid
+   (\mathtt a_1\xi_1^1\mathtt b_1,
+    \mathtt c_1\xi_1^2)(A_1),\\
+ A_i&\to(\mathtt a_i,\mathtt b_i\mathtt{\#}_i)
+   \mid
+   (\mathtt a_i\xi_1^1,\mathtt b_i\xi_1^2)(A_i)
+   \qquad(2\le i\le4),\\
+ B_4&\to(\xi_1^1\xi_1^2)(A_4),\\
+ B_i&\to(\xi_1^1\xi_1^2\xi_2^1)(A_i,B_{i+1})
+   \qquad(1\le i\le3),\\
+ S&\to(\mathtt{\#}_0\xi_1^1)(B_1).
+\end{aligned}
+\]
+The $A_1$-component derives
+$(\mathtt a_1^n\mathtt b_1^n,
+  \mathtt c_1^n\mathtt{\#}_1)$, while for $i\ge2$,
+$A_i$ derives
+$(\mathtt a_i^n,\mathtt b_i^n\mathtt{\#}_i)$.
+The $B_i$ rules concatenate these blocks from right to left.  Thus the grammar
+generates exactly $L_{\mathrm{r2}}$, has fan-out at most two, and has maximum
+rule rank two.
+
+Next consider the regular skeleton
+\[
+\begin{aligned}
+ Q_{\mathrm{r2}}
+ :={}&
+ \mathtt{\#}_0\mathtt a_1^+\mathtt b_1^+\mathtt c_1^+\mathtt{\#}_1
+ \mathtt a_2^+\mathtt b_2^+\mathtt{\#}_2\\
+ &{}\mathtt a_3^+\mathtt b_3^+\mathtt{\#}_3
+ \mathtt a_4^+\mathtt b_4^+\mathtt{\#}_4.
+\end{aligned}
+\]
+Because the block alphabets and separator symbols are distinct and occur in a
+fixed order, $Q_{\mathrm{r2}}$ is strictly $2$-local: its permitted adjacent
+pairs together with the two end-marker pairs determine exactly the displayed
+skeleton.  Define a homomorphism
+$\varphi:\Sigma_{\mathrm{r2}}^*\to\mathbb Z^5$ by
+\[
+ \varphi(w)=
+ \bigl(
+ |w|_{\mathtt a_1}-|w|_{\mathtt b_1},
+ |w|_{\mathtt b_1}-|w|_{\mathtt c_1},
+ |w|_{\mathtt a_2}-|w|_{\mathtt b_2},
+ |w|_{\mathtt a_3}-|w|_{\mathtt b_3},
+ |w|_{\mathtt a_4}-|w|_{\mathtt b_4}
+ \bigr).
+\]
+Then
+\[
+ L_{\mathrm{r2}}=Q_{\mathrm{r2}}\cap\varphi^{-1}(0).
+\]
+Lemma~\ref{lem:sl2-boundary-control} and
+Proposition~\ref{prop:abelian-coset-filter}(ii) therefore give
+\[
+ L_{\mathrm{r2}}\in\Cmcf{2}{h_{\Sigma_{\mathrm{r2}}}^{1,1}}.
+\]
+
+For the rank lower bound, let $\pi$ erase $\mathtt c_1$, send each distinct
+separator $\mathtt{\#}_j$ to a single symbol $\mathtt{\#}$, and fix all
+$\mathtt a_i,\mathtt b_i$.  Then
+\[
+\begin{aligned}
+ \pi(L_{\mathrm{r2}})
+ =K_2:=\bigl\{\, &
+ \mathtt{\#}\mathtt a_1^{n_1}\mathtt b_1^{n_1}
+ \mathtt{\#}\mathtt a_2^{n_2}\mathtt b_2^{n_2}
+ \mathtt{\#}\mathtt a_3^{n_3}\mathtt b_3^{n_3}\\
+ &{}\mathtt{\#}\mathtt a_4^{n_4}\mathtt b_4^{n_4}\mathtt{\#}
+ :n_1,n_2,n_3,n_4\ge1
+ \,\bigr\}.
+\end{aligned}
+\]
+This is the standard four-block separator language recorded by Kato, Seki,
+and Kasami as a member of
+$\mathrm{CFL}\setminus\mathrm{SL\mbox{-}TAL}$
+\cite{KatoSekiKasami2004}.  Kato subsequently proved
+\[
+ \mathrm{SL\mbox{-}TAL}=\mathsf{MCFL}(2,1)
+\]
+\cite[Theorem~18]{Kato2005Thesis}.  If $L_{\mathrm{r2}}$ had a fan-out-two
+rank-one MCFG presentation, applying $\pi$ to every terminal occurrence in
+its rule templates would preserve fan-out and rule rank and would yield a
+fan-out-two rank-one presentation of $K_2$, a contradiction.  Hence
+$L_{\mathrm{r2}}\notin\mathsf{MCFL}(2,1)$.
+
+Finally, $L_{\mathrm{r2}}$ is not Yoshinaka-substitutable already at arity
+one.  Put
+\[
+ T:=
+ \mathtt{\#}_1\mathtt a_2\mathtt b_2\mathtt{\#}_2
+ \mathtt a_3\mathtt b_3\mathtt{\#}_3
+ \mathtt a_4\mathtt b_4\mathtt{\#}_4,
+\]
+and take
+\[
+ x:=\mathtt b_1,\qquad
+ y:=\mathtt a_1\mathtt b_1^2\mathtt c_1,
+\]
+with one-hole sentence contexts
+\[
+ E:=\mathtt{\#}_0\mathtt a_1\blank_1\mathtt c_1T,
+ \qquad
+ F:=\mathtt{\#}_0\mathtt a_1^2\mathtt b_1
+       \blank_1\mathtt c_1^2T.
+\]
+Then $E[x],E[y],F[x]\in L_{\mathrm{r2}}$, corresponding respectively to
+$n_1=1,2,2$, whereas
+$F[y]\notin L_{\mathrm{r2}}$ because an $\mathtt a_1$ occurs after the
+$\mathtt b_1$ block has begun.  This violates the substitution implication,
+so $L_{\mathrm{r2}}\notin\YSub{2}$.
+\end{proof}
+
+The role of the typing in the last argument is visible directly:
+$h_{\Sigma_{\mathrm{r2}}}^{1,1}(x)$ records the one-letter word
+$\mathtt b_1$, whereas
+$h_{\Sigma_{\mathrm{r2}}}^{1,1}(y)$ records first letter $\mathtt a_1$ and
+last letter $\mathtt c_1$.  The typed learner therefore does not license the
+substitution that defeats Yoshinaka's trivial-typing condition.
+
